---

<p>
  As we move into an era of shorter and shorter content & attention span, it's
  becoming harder to appeal to users.
</p>
<h2>Design principle</h2>
<p>
  With a short attention span the first idea would be to pack as much
  information as possible in a single view, but that would overwhelm many users,
  so we can't even have a gigantic wall of text.
</p>
<h3>So what?</h3>
<p>Well, we need:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Show as much as possible</li>
  <li>Use as little text as possible</li>
</ul>
<p>
  With this in mind, I got to work in September 2026, I started by asking anyone
  I knew the following:
</p>
<ul>
  <li class="italic">"What's the app you open most often?"</li>
  <li class="italic">"What makes you use that app?"</li>
  <li class="italic">"Which parts of the app you like the least?"</li>
</ul>
<p>From this pool of people:</p>
<ul>
  <li>10 office workers</li>
  <li>8 developers</li>
  <li>25 students</li>
</ul>
<p>
  I got reviews of the whole "Office365" suite, VSCode, Atom (unfortunately
  gone), GitHub, Instagram, TikTok, and many more apps;<br />one thing became
  clear: the bigger the task, the harder the interface is to use.
</p>
<p>
  So I got to work, and I discovered different techniques of packing data, down
  here I've divided it by end-target.
</p>
<h2>Critical infrastructure reporting</h2>
<p>
  On any infrastructure we need to handle faults, warnings and incidents
  efficiently, and in 99% of cases you want something immediate that tells you
  either "it's all good" or "something's wrong"
</p>
<p>
  The end goal therefore is something along the lines of having this starting
  view:
</p>

<VStack
  as="section"
  aria-label="Example infrastructure fault interface"
  align="center"
  justify="center"
  style={{
    minHeight: "320px",
    padding: "32px 20px",
    marginBlock: "24px",
    borderRadius: "16px",
    backgroundColor: "#000",
  }}
>
  <HStack align="center" justify="center" gap="md">
    <Circle diameter={24} fill="oklch(64.5% 0.246 16.439)" />
    <Text
      color="#fff"
      className="flex"
      style={{
        fontFamily: "var(--ui-font-family)",
        fontSize: "clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem)",
        fontWeight: 600,
        lineHeight: 1.1,
        textAlign: "center",
      }}
    >
      1 Fault detected</Text
    >
  </HStack>
  <p>On <Badge tone="soft" variant="danger">main-prod-server-01</Badge></p>
  <Button client:load variant="secondary" radius="full"
    ><HStack align="center" justify="center">Details <CaretRightIcon /></HStack
    ></Button
  >
</VStack>

<p>
  It's simple, straight to the point, it can be fitted anywhere in a dashboard
  and doesn't overwhelm the user, only when he actually needs to know what's
  going on the "Details" button helps him get there
</p>
<p>
  This is especially useful when a non technical user accesses the platform,
  it's not telling him exactly what's wrong, but in his mind he'll think "I
  better call IT for this".
</p>
<p>Similarly, when everything is going fine:</p>

